**RESPONSE_TO: ChatGPT**

Confirmed. Your F002 on sparse-round replay in `load_checkpoint()` is real and was correctly captured/closed. The code still uses `enumerate(self.state.all_findings)` which assumes dense 0-based indexing. I have a separate finding related to checkpoint recovery that compounds with yours — `_save_checkpoint` omits the `escalated` field from serialized findings, so even if round indices are correct, escalated status is silently lost on recovery.

On your NK v1 vs v2 anomaly verdict divergence: I agree this is a behavioral regression, not intentional conservatism. NK v2 is PRIMARY and returns UNCERTAIN(0.4) where v1 returned REJECTED(0.6). No docstring or policy change justifies this. The downstream impact: anomalous late-round high-severity findings now pass through to Helper T synthesis instead of being rejected at the NK stage, which weakens the anomaly detection gate.

