    "DESCRIPTION": "FIND: `bench/immune_agents.py`, `_reconciliation_gate()` near-tie handling assigns MAX confidence to UNCERTAIN verdicts. Evidence: when `margin = abs(v1c - v2c) < 0.10`, the code sets `reconciled_conf[fid] = max(v1c, v2c)`. For CONFIRMED(0.85) vs REJECTED(0.80), the result is UNCERTAIN(0.85). This conflates 'the verdict is uncertain' with 'we are 85% confident'. Downstream, `ImmuneResponse.final_confidences` for UNCERTAIN findings will show high values, misleading any consumer that interprets confidence as verdict strength. The Regulatory T cell counts UNCERTAIN verdicts by count (not confidence-weighted), so the immediate pipeline impact is limited, but the semantic contract of the confidence field is violated: UNCERTAIN with 0.85 confidence is incoherent.",
    "PROPOSED_FIX": "FIX: Set confidence to the margin itself (how close the disagreement was), bounded to [0, 0.5].\n\n<<<<\n            if margin < 0.10:\n                reconciled[fid] = \"UNCERTAIN\"\n                reconciled_conf[fid] = max(v1c, v2c)\n====\n            if margin < 0.10:\n                reconciled[fid] = \"UNCERTAIN\"\n                reconciled_conf[fid] = round(margin, 4)  # Low confidence = close call\n>>>>",
    "VERIFIED": false,
    "FOLLOW": "FOLLOW: With margin-based confidence, near-ties produce UNCERTAIN(0.03) instead of UNCERTAIN(0.85). This correctly communicates 'the pipelines nearly agreed but not quite, so the verdict is genuinely uncertain'. If downstream consumers ever weight UNCERTAIN verdicts by confidence (e.g., for tiebreaking or prioritization), this produces sane behavior. The current pipeline doesn't weight UNCERTAINs, so this is a semantic/interface fix rather than a functional one."
  }
]
