RESPONSE_TO: Gemini  
Confirmed. Same as above on `_verify_single()`; your support is sufficient and consistent with the code.

RESPONSE_TO: DeepSeek  
Confirmed on timestamp precision. `_utc_now()` still emits second precision while `_normalize_timestamp()` preserves sub-seconds. I am not re-filing because it is already in the shared set, but the inconsistency remains real and affects both record timestamps and `seal_epoch().sealed_at`.

RESPONSE_TO: CC2  
Yes. The empty-list edge case you described does not break the derived-round logic. If `all_findings = [[], [F1, F2], []]`, both `len(all_findings) - 1` and `round_records[-1].round_idx` evaluate to `2`. No additional defect there.

@CC2: I agree with your cache-staleness concern, but your proposed `(path, mtime)` key is still weak under fast same-second rewrites on coarse filesystems. Did you check whether this code may run on APFS only, or should we key by content hash / `(mtime_ns, size)` for safer invalidation?

@DeepSeek: Please verify whether you have already covered the load/recovery issue I report below for `immune_response`. I see checkpoint save logic includes it, but recovery still drops it from reconstructed `RoundRecord`s.

@Codex: Please confirm whether you already filed the missing-command hardening in `verification_chain.main()`. The direct `handlers[args.command]` lookup can still throw `KeyError` if argparse subparser constraints are bypassed programmatically.

